s digit, hundreds digit, tens digit, units digit.\n\nThousands digit: all numbers are 1xxx, 2xxx, 3xxx \u2014 never 7. So 0 from thousands.\n\nHundreds digit: count multiples of 11 or 13 in [1000,4000) with hundreds digit 7, i.e., in ranges [1700,1800), [2700,2800), [3700,3800).\n\nMultiples of 11 in [1700, 1800): 11\u00d7155 = 1705, ..., 11\u00d7163 = 1793. Count: 163 - 155 + 1 = 9.\nMultiples of 13 in [1700, 1800): 13\u00d7131 = 1703, ..., 13\u00d7138 = 1794. Count: 138 - 131 + 1 = 8.\nMultiples of 143 in [1700, 1800): 143\u00d712 = 1716. Count: 1.\nUnion: 9 + 8 - 1 = 16.\n\nMultiples of 11 in [2700, 2800): 11\u00d7246 = 2706, ..., 11\u00d7254 = 2794. Count: 9.\nMultiples of 13 in [2700, 2800): 13\u00d7208 = 2704, ..., 13\u00d7215 = 2795. Count: 8.\nMultiples of 143 in [2700, 2800): 143\u00d719 = 2717. Count: 1.\nUnion: 9 + 8 - 1 = 16.\n\nMultiples of 11 in [3700, 3800): 11\u00d7337 = 3707, ..., 11\u00d7345 = 3795. Count: 9.\nMultiples of 13 in [3700, 3800): 13\u00d7285 = 3705, ..., 13\u00d7292 = 3796. Count: 8.\nMultiples of 143 in [3700, 3800): 143\u00d726 = 3718. Count: 1.\nUnion: 16.\n\nHundreds digit '7' count: 16 \u00d7 3 = 48.\n\nTens digit: count multiples with tens digit 7, i.e., numbers of form x y 7 z where the number \u2261 0 mod 11 or 13. For each hundreds-block [ab00, ab00+100), count multiples of 11 or 13 in [ab70, ab80).\n\nFor multiples of 11: in any 100 consecutive integers, there are either 9 or 10 multiples of 11 (since 100/11 \u2248 9.09). In a specific 10-wide window [ab70, ab80), the count of multiples of 11 is either 0 or 1 (10 < 11). Similarly for 13: 0 or 1.\n\nSo for each of the 30 hundreds-blocks (hundreds digit 0-2 for thousands 1-3... wait, thousands 1, 2, 3 and hundreds 0-9 \u2192 30 blocks), count multiples of 11 in [ab70, ab80) plus multiples of 13 in [ab70, ab80) minus multiples of 143 in [ab70, ab80).\n\nMultiples of 11 in [ab70, ab80): the number 11k \u2261 ab70 mod 100... Let me think: multiples of 11 mod 100 cycle with period 11: 0, 11, 22, ..., 99, 0, ... Actually multiples of 11 mod 100: 11k mod 100 for k = 0..: 0, 11, 22, 33, 44, 55, 66, 77, 88, 99, 10, 21, 32, ... The last two digits of multiples of 11 take values \u2261 0 mod 11 (mod 100)? No wait: 11k mod 100. Since gcd(11, 100) = 1, 11k mod 100 cycles through all residues as k ranges over 0..99. So in any window of 10 consecutive integers [ab70, ab80), exactly... hmm, the multiples of 11 in a window of 10 consecutive integers: since 11 > 10, there's at most 1, and there's 1 iff the window contains a multiple of 11. The window [ab70, ab80) contains a multiple of 11 iff there exists k with ab70 \u2264 11k < ab80, i.e., 11k mod 100 \u2208 [70, 80). Since 11k mod 100 hits every residue class... over k = 0..99, 11k mod 100 takes each value exactly once. So for the tens digit to be 7: 11k mod 100 \u2208 {70, 71, ..., 79}. That's 10 values out of 100, so 10% of multiples of 11 have tens digit 7? Wait, but that's not right either \u2014 11k mod 100 for k = 0..99 gives all 100 residues, so exactly 10 values of k (out of every 100 consecutive k) give tens digit 7. Hmm, but that means 10% of multiples of 11 have tens digit 7? Let me sanity check with the 3-digit case: multiples of 11 in [100, 1000): 110 to 990, that's 81 multiples. Tens digit 7: numbers ending in 7x: 176, 187? No wait, tens digit 7 means the number is like x77? No \u2014 tens digit is the second-to-last digit. 176: digits 1,7,6 \u2014 tens digit is 7. Numbers with tens digit 7: 170-179, 270-279, ..., 970-979. Multiples of 11 in these: 176, 187? No, 187 has tens digit 8. Let me list: 176 (1,7,6 \u2014 tens 7 \u2713), 275, 374, 473, 572, 671, 770, 781? No, 781 tens digit is 8. Hmm wait: 770 \u2192 digits 7,7,0, tens digit 7 \u2713. 781 \u2192 7,8,1, tens 8 \u2717. Let me list multiples of 11 with tens digit 7 in [100,1000): 176, 275, 374, 473, 572, 671, 770, 869, 968. That's 9. And 81 multiples total \u2192 9/81 = 1/9 \u2248 11.1%. Hmm, not exactly 10%.\n\nOK here's the thing: 11k mod 100 cycles through all residues with period 100 in k. Multiples of 11 in [100, 1000) correspond to k in [10, 91) (since 11\u00d710 = 110, 11\u00d790 = 990, so k = 10..90, 81 values). For tens digit 7: 11k mod 100 \u2208 [70, 80). 11k mod 100 = (11k) mod 100. Let me solve: 11k \u2261 r mod 100 where r \u2208 [70, 80). k \u2261 11^{-1} r mod 100. 11 \u00d7 91 = 1001 \u2261 1 mod 100, so 11^{-1} = 91. k \u2261 91r mod 100. For r = 70: k \u2261 91\u00d770 = 6370 \u2261 70 mod 100. For r = 71: 91\u00d771 = 6461 \u2261 61. r=72: 91\u00d772 = 6552 \u2261 52. r=73: 6643 \u2261 43. r=74: 6734 \u2261 34. r=75: 6825 \u2261 25. r=76: 6916 \u2261 16. r=77: 7007 \u2261 7. r=78: 7098 \u2261 98. r=79: 7189 \u2261 89.\n\nSo k mod 100 \u2208 {70, 61, 52, 43, 34, 25, 16, 7, 98, 89} for tens digit 7. In k \u2208 [10, 91): values 16, 25, 34, 43, 52, 61, 70, 89 \u2192 8 values? Wait: 7 < 10, 98 \u2265 91, 89 \u2265 91? 89 < 91 \u2713. So k \u2208 {16, 25, 34, 43, 52, 61, 70, 89} \u2192 8 values. Hmm, but I listed 9 numbers above: 176 (k=16), 275 (k=25), 374 (k=34), 473 (k=43), 572 (k=52), 671 (k=61), 770 (k=70), 869 (k=79)? Wait, 869 = 11 \u00d7 79. 11 \u00d7 79 = 869. k=79. But 79 is not in my set {16, 25, 34, 43, 52, 61, 70, 89}. Let me recheck: 869 mod 100 = 69, tens digit 6, not 7! I made an error. 869: digits 8, 6, 9. Tens digit is 6. Oops. And 968 = 11 \u00d7 88, 968 mod 100 = 68, tens digit 6. I made errors in my listing.\n\nLet me redo: multiples of 11 with tens digit 7 in [100, 1000): k such that 11k \u2208 [100, 1000) and (11k // 10) % 10 == 7. From the k set {16, 25, 34, 43, 52, 61, 70, 89}: 11\u00d716 = 176 \u2713, 11\u00d725 = 275 \u2713, 11\u00d734 = 374 \u2713, 11\u00d743 = 473 \u2713, 11\u00d752 = 572 \u2713, 11\u00d761 = 671 \u2713, 11\u00d770 = 770 \u2713, 11\u00d789 = 979 \u2713. So 8 numbers: 176, 275, 374, 473, 572, 671, 770, 979.\n\nOK so this positional counting approach works but is getting complex. Let me just carefully count for [1000, 4000).\n\nActuall