 100 \u2208 [70, 80) \u2192 k mod 100 \u2208 {70, 61, 52, 43, 34, 25, 16, 7, 98, 89} (computed above). For ab from 10 to 39: 11k \u2208 [ab70, ab80) means k \u2208 [ab70/11, ab80/11). Alternatively: for each ab, check if any k \u2261 r (mod 100) with r in the set gives 11k \u2208 [ab70, ab80).\n\nSimpler: for each ab in 10..39, the window is [100\u00d7ab + 70, 100\u00d7ab + 80). A multiple of 11 is in this window iff 11k mod 100 \u2208 [70, 80) and floor(11k/100) = ab. Since 11k mod 100 determines k mod 100, and each ab corresponds to k in a range of ~9 values... Let me just directly compute for each ab whether there's a multiple of 11 in [ab70, ab80).\n\nThe multiples of 11 near ab70: 11k where k = round(ab70/11)... Let me compute 11k mod 1000 pattern? Hmm, alternative: multiples of 11 in [ab70, ab80): the number ab70 + j for j = 0..9. ab70 + j \u2261 0 mod 11. ab70 mod 11: ab70 = 100\u00d7ab + 70. 100 \u2261 1 mod 11, so ab70 \u2261 ab + 70 \u2261 ab + 4 mod 11 (since 70 = 66 + 4). So ab70 + j \u2261 ab + 4 + j mod 11. We need ab + 4 + j \u2261 0 mod 11 for some j \u2208 [0, 10). j = (-ab - 4) mod 11. This j must be \u2264 9. So there's a multiple of 11 in the window iff (-ab - 4) mod 11 \u2264 9, i.e., (-ab - 4) mod 11 \u2260 10.\n\n(-ab - 4) mod 11 = 10 iff ab + 4 \u2261 1 mod 11 iff ab \u2261 8 mod 11 (since -ab - 4 \u2261 10 \u2192 ab + 4 \u2261 -10 \u2261 1 mod 11 \u2192 ab \u2261 -3 \u2261 8 mod 11).\n\nSo for ab \u2262 8 (mod 11), there's exactly one multiple of 11 in [ab70, ab80); for ab \u2261 8 (mod 11), none.\n\nab ranges over 10..39: ab \u2261 8 mod 11 \u2192 ab \u2208 {19, 30} (8, 19, 30, 41 \u2014 within 10..39: 19, 30). So 28 blocks have a multiple of 11 with tens digit 7, 2 blocks don't. Count of multiples of 11 with tens digit 7 in [1000, 4000): 28.\n\nWait, but I need to double check: j = (-ab - 4) mod 11 must be in [0, 10), i.e., j \u2264 9. j = 10 means no multiple. j = (-ab-4) mod 11 = 10 iff ab \u2261 8 mod 11. Let me verify with an example: 