id state pollution \u2014 since the function is pure, there's no state to pollute. I could still note that.\n\nAlso, to be thorough about branch coverage:\n- Line 4 loop: entered (n > 0) and not entered (n = 0).\n- Line 5 condition: True (i=0, 11, 13) and False (i=1, etc.). Also the `or` short-circuit: i % 11 == 0 True (i=11) without evaluating i % 13; i % 11 != 0 but i % 13 == 0 (i=13); both false (i=1).\n- Line 9 loop: entered (s non-empty) and not entered (s empty, n=0 case).\n\nLet me also add a test with n=1: range(1) \u2192 i=0, 0 % 11 == 0 \u2192 True, append 0. s = \"0\". Loop over \"0\": ans += (0 == '7') \u2192 False \u2192 0. Return 0. This covers line 10 with False.\n\nFor line 10 with True: fizz_buzz(78) covers it.\n\nActually, let me think about whether I should use get_info tool. The instructions say \"Use the get_info tool function as necessary.\" I could check the function signature. Let me call get_info on \"fizz_buzz\" to confirm. Actually, it might be useful to verify the module structure. Let me do that.\n\nActually, the code is given, so I know the signature. But the tool might reveal the module name or how it's importable. Let me call get_info(\"fizz_buzz\") to see.\n\nI'll make the call and then write the tests.\n\nLet me write the final test script:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Tests for f036.fizz_buzz (HumanEval #36).\n\nfizz_buzz(n) counts the number of times the digit 7 appears in the\nconcatenation of all integers in [0, n) that are divisible by 11 or 13.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom f036 import fizz_buzz\n\n\ndef test_n_zero_skips_all_loops():\n    # n == 0: the `for i in range(n)` loop body never runs and the\n    # final `for c in s` loop never runs either (s is the empty string).\n    assert fizz_buzz(0) == 0\n\n\ndef test_single_iteration_zero_is_multiple():\n    # n == 1: loop runs once with i == 0, which satisfies\n    # `i % 11 == 0` (first operand of the `or`), so 0 is appended.\n    # s == \"0\" so the character loop runs, but no '7' is counted.\n    assert fizz_buzz(1) == 0\n\n\ndef test_condition_false_branch():\n    # i == 1 satisfies neither `i % 11 == 0` nor `i % 13 == 0`,\n    # exercising the False branch of the condition.\n    assert fizz_buzz(2) == 0\n\n\ndef test_divisible_by_13_only():\n    # 13 is divisible by 13 but not by 11, exercising the second\n    # operand of the `or` after the first one is False.\n    assert fizz_buzz(14) == 0  # s == \"013\" -> no '7'\n\n\ndef test_divisible_by_11_only():\n    # 11 is divisible by 11 but not by 13, exercising the first\n    # operand of the `or` being True (short-circuit).\n    assert fizz_buzz(12) == 0  # s == \"011\" -> no '7'\n\n\ndef test_counts_sevens():\n    # Multiples of 11 or 13 below 78: 0, 11, 13, 22, 26, 33, 39, 44,\n    # 52, 55, 65, 66, 77 -> \"01113223339445255656677\" has two '7's.\n    assert fizz_buzz(78) == 2\n\n\ndef test_counts_sevens_includes_78():\n    # 78 (divisible by 13) adds one more '7' -> three in total.\n    assert fizz_buzz(79) == 3\n\n\ndef test_larger_input():\n    # Known reference value for HumanEval #36.\n    assert fizz_buzz(100) == 3\n    assert fizz_buzz(200) == 6\n    assert fizz_buzz(4000) == 192\n    assert fizz_buzz(10000) == 639\n```\n\nWait, let me double check fizz_buzz(4000) == 192 and fizz_buzz(10000) == 639. These are from the HumanEval test suite, so they should be correct. Let me verify manually-ish.\n\nActually, let me compute fizz_buzz(4000). Numbers < 4000 divisible by 11 or 13. The concatenation of these numbers, count '7's.\n\nHmm, this is tedious but let me try to verify with a different approach. Actually, the HumanEval canonical solution:\n\n```python\ndef fizz_buzz(n: int):\n    ns = []\n    for i in range(n):\n        if i % 11 == 0 or i % 13 == 0:\n            ns.append(i)\n    s = ''.join(list(map(str, ns)))\n    ans = 0\n    for c in s:\n        ans += (c == '7')\n    return ans\n```\n\nAnd the tests in HumanEval:\n```\nfizz_buzz(50) == 0\nfizz_buzz(78) == 2\nfizz_buzz(79) == 3\nfizz_buzz(100) == 3\nfizz_buzz(200) == 6\nfizz_buzz(4000) == 192\nfizz_buzz(10000) == 639\nfizz_buzz(99999) == 8026\nfizz_buzz(0) == 0\n```\n\nThese are the official test values, so they're correct. I'll include a few of them.\n\nActually, let me verify fizz_buzz(4000) == 192 by reasoning. Count of numbers < 4000 divisible by 11: floor(3999/11) + 1 = 363 + 1 = 364 (including 0). Divisible by 13: floor(3999/13) + 1 = 307 + 1 = 308. Divisible by both (143): floor(3999/143) + 1 = 27 + 1 = 28. Total = 364 + 308 - 28 = 644 numbers. Total digits: numbers 0-99 that are multiples: 0, 11, 13, 22, 26, 33, 39, 44, 52, 55, 65, 66, 77, 78, 88, 91 \u2192 16 numbers, 1-2 digits each: 0 (1 digit), 11,13,22,26,33,39,44,52,55,65,66,77,78,88,91 (15 numbers \u00d7 2 digits = 30). Total 31 digits for < 100.\n\nThis is getting tedious. I'll trust the HumanEval reference values. Actually, to be safe, maybe I should compute them programmatically in the test? No \u2014 that would just re-implement the function. Better to use known expected values.\n\nAlternatively, I can compute the expected values by hand for smaller cases and use the HumanEval reference values for larger ones. The HumanEval values are well-established.\n\nLet me verify fizz_buzz(200) == 6 by hand:\nMultiples of 11 or 13 below 200:\n11, 22, 33, 44, 55, 66, 77, 88, 99, 110, 121, 132, 143, 154, 165, 176, 187, 198 (multiples of 11)\n13, 26, 39, 52, 65, 78, 91, 104, 117, 130, 143, 156, 169, 182, 195 (multiples of 13)\n143 is common.\nUnion: 0, 11, 13, 22, 26, 33, 39, 44, 52, 55, 65, 66, 77, 78, 88, 91, 99, 104, 110, 117, 121, 130, 132, 143, 154, 156, 165, 169, 176, 182, 187, 195, 198.\n\nConcatenated string: \"0\" \"11\" \"13\" \"22\" \"26\" \"33\" \"39\" \"44\" \"52\" \"55\" \"65\" \"66\" \"77\" \"78\" \"88\" \"91\" \"99\" \"104\" \"110\" \"117\" \"121\" \"130\" \"132\" \"143\" \"154\" \"156\" \"165\" \"169\" \"176\" \"182\" \"187\" \"195\" \"198\"\n\nCount '7's:\n- 0: no\n- 11: no\n- 13: no\n- 22: no\n- 26: no\n- 33: no\n- 39: no\n- 44: no\n- 52: no\n- 55: no\n- 65: no\n- 66: no\n- 77: two 7s \u2192 2\n- 78: one 7 \u2192 3\n- 88: no\n- 91: no\n- 99: no\n- 104: no\n- 110: no\n- 117: one 7 \u2192 4\n- 121: no\n- 130: no\n- 132: no\n- 143: no\n- 154: no\n- 156: no\n- 165: no\n- 169: no\n- 176: one 7 \u2192 5\n- 182: no\n- 187: one 7 \u2192 6\n- 195: no\n- 198: no\n\nTotal: 6. \u2713\n\nfizz_buzz(100) == 3: multiples below 100: 0, 11, 13, 22, 26, 33, 39, 44, 52, 55, 65, 66, 77, 78, 88, 91. '7's: 77 \u2192 2, 78 \u2192 1. Total 3. \u2713\n\nfizz_buzz(79) == 3: adds 78 \u2192 3. \u2713\nfizz_buzz(78) == 2: up to 77 \u2192 2. \u2713\nfizz_buzz(50) == 0: multiples below 50: 0, 11, 13, 22, 26, 33, 39, 44. No '7'. \u2713\n\nNow, about the import: the code is \"extracted from f036/__init__.py\", so the module is `f036` and the function is `f036.fizz_buzz`. Import: `from f036 import fizz_buzz`.\n\nShould I use get_info first? The instructions say \"Use the get_info tool function as necessary.\" It might be prudent to check the function exists and its signature. Let me call get_info(\"fizz_buzz\").\n\nSince the function is pure (no side effects, no file I/O, no global state), there's no cleanup needed. But the instructions emphasize cleanup \u2014 I can note that the function is pure so no monkeypatching is needed. Actually, maybe I shouldn't add unnecessary monkeypatching. The instruction says \"use 'monkeypatch' or 'pytest-mock' if appropriate\" \u2014 it's not appropriate here since there's no state.\n\nLet me also think about branch coverage more carefully:\n\nLines:\n- 3: `ns = []` \u2014 any call.\n- 4: `for i in range(n):` \u2014 loop with n=0 (skip) and n>0 (enter).\n- 5: `if i % 11 == 0 or i % 13 == 0:` \u2014 branches:\n  - i % 11 == 0 True \u2192 short-circuit (i=0, 11, 22, ...)\n  - i % 11 != 0, i % 13 == 0 \u2192 True (i=13, 26, ...)\n  - both False (i=1, 2, ...)\n- 6: `ns.append(i)` \u2014 when condition True.\n- 7: `s = ''.join(...)` \u2014 any call.\n- 8: `ans = 0` \u2014 any call.\n- 9: `for c in s:` \u2014 s empty (n=0) and s non-empty.\n- 10: `ans += (c == '7')` \u2014 c == '7' True and False.\n- 11: `return ans` \u2014 any call.\n\nMy tests cover all of these:\n- test_n_zero_skips_all_loops: n=0 \u2192 lines 3, 7, 8, 11; loops skipped.\n- test_single_iteration_zero_is_multiple: n=1 \u2192 i=0, condition True via first operand, append, s=\"0\", loop over \"0\" with c != '7'.\n- test_condition_false_branch: n=2 \u2192 i=1, both operands False.\n- test_divisible_by_13_only: n=14 \u2192 i=13, first operand False, second True.\n- test_divisible_by_11_only: n=12 \u2192 i=11, first operand True (short-circuit).\n- test_counts_sevens: n=78 \u2192 c == '7' True.\n- etc.\n\nAll branches covered. \n\nOne more consideration: should the tests be in one function or multiple? Multiple small tests are fine and idiomatic. The instruction says \"Create new pytest test functions\" (plural), so multiple functions are good.\n\nAlso \"Write as little top-level code as possible\" \u2014 just imports and test functions.\n\nLet me also consider: maybe I should verify with get_info that the function is importable as `f036.fizz_buzz`. The tool returns \"information about a symbol\". Let me call it with \"fizz_buzz\".\n\nI'll make the call now. Since there's only one symbol to check, one call suffices. Actually, maybe I should also check the module itself? get_info takes \"class, function or method name\". Let me just check \"fizz_buzz\".\n\nAfter the call, I'll write the final test script.\n\nLet me draft the final script:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Tests for f036.fizz_buzz.\n\nfizz_buzz(n) returns the number of times the digit 7 appears in the\nconcatenated decimal representations of all integers i in [0, n) that\nare divisible by 11 or by 13.\n\nThe function under test is pure (no I/O, no globals, no side effects),\nso no monkeypatching or teardown is required; each test leaves no\nstate behind.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom f036 import fizz_buzz\n\n\ndef test_n_zero_skips_both_loops():\n    \"\"\"n == 0: neither the range loop nor the character loop executes.\"\"\"\n    assert fizz_buzz(0) == 0\n\n\ndef test_zero_is_multiple_of_both():\n    \"\"\"n == 1: i == 0 satisfies `i % 11 == 0` (first `or` operand).\"\"\"\n    # s == \"0\": character loop runs, but '0' != '7'.\n    assert fizz_buzz(1) == 0\n\n\ndef test_condition_false_branch():\n    \"\"\"i == 1 satisfies neither operand of the `or`.\"\"\"\n    assert fizz_buzz(2) == 0\n\n\ndef test_divisible_by_11_only():\n    \"\"\"i == 11: first operand True, second never evaluated.\"\"\"\n    assert fizz_buzz(12) == 0  # s == \"011\" contains no '7'\n\n\ndef test_divisible_by_13_only():\n    \"\"\"i == 13: first operand False, second True.\"\"\"\n    assert fizz_buzz(14) == 0  # s == \"013\" contains no '7'\n\n\ndef test_counts_sevens_before_78():\n    \"\"\"Multiples of 11/13 below 78 concatenate to ...'77'; two 7s.\"\"\"\n    assert fizz_buzz(78) == 2\n\n\ndef test_counts_sevens_including_78():\n    \"\"\"78 is divisible by 13 and contributes one more '7'.\"\"\"\n    assert fizz_buzz(79) == 3\n\n\ndef test_reference_values():\n    \"\"\"Known-good reference values for larger inputs.\"\"\"\n    assert fizz_buzz(50) == 0\n    assert fizz_buzz(100) == 3\n    assert fizz_buzz(200) == 6\n    assert fizz_buzz(4000) == 192\n    assert fizz_buzz(10000) == 639\n```\n\nHmm, should I include pytest import? Not strictly needed if I don't use pytest.raises or fixtures. But it's harmless. Actually, \"Write as little top-level code as possible\" \u2014 I'll skip the unused pytest import. Actually, some linters complain about unused imports; better to omit it since I don't use any pytest features.\n\nWait \u2014 should I double-check fizz_buzz(4000) == 192? Let me try to verify computationally in my head... that's really hard. Let me instead reason about it differently.\n\nActually, I can verify by writing out the computation logic mentally in chunks. Let me count numbers < 4000 divisible by 11 or 13, and count '7' digits in their concatenation.\n\nMultiples of 11 below 4000: 0, 11, ..., 3993. Count: 3993/11 = 363, so 364 numbers (0 through 363 \u00d7 11).\nMultiples of 13 below 4000: 0, 13, ..., 3991. 3991/13 = 307, so 308 numbers.\nMultiples of 143 below 4000: 0, 143, ..., 399... 143 \u00d7 27 = 3861, 143 \u00d7 28 = 4004 > 4000. So 0..27 \u2192 28 numbers.\nUnion count: 364 + 308 - 28 = 644 numbers.\n\nNow count '7' digits in concatenation. This requires knowing each number's digits. Total is 644 numbers; digits per number vary from 1 to 4.\n\nThis is too tedious to do by hand reliably. The HumanEval dataset's test values are canonical and widely used; fizz_buzz(4000) == 192 is one of them. I'm confident in these values. Similarly 10000 \u2192 639.\n\nActually, let me sanity check 10000 \u2192 639 roughly. Numbers < 10000 divisible by 11 or 13: multiples of 11: 0..9999 \u2192 floor(9999/11)=909, +1 = 910. Multiples of 13: floor(9999/13) = 769, +1 = 770. Multiples of 143: floor(9999/143) = 69, +1 = 70. Union: 910 + 770 - 70 = 1610 numbers.\n\nDigit distribution: For a rough estimate, count of '7' digits among these numbers... The expected count of digit 7 in random numbers: each digit position has 1/10 chance. Total digits \u2248 1610 \u00d7 ~3.5 \u2248 5600, expected 7s \u2248 560. But there are biases: multiples of 11 have patterns (e.g., alternating sum), and 77, 777, etc. 639 is plausible.\n\nHmm, but let me think about whether there's a bias. Multiples of 11 below 10000: last two digits... Actually, multiples of 11 have the property that alternating digit sum \u2261 0 mod 11. For 2-digit multiples of 11: 11, 22, ..., 99 \u2014 all have repeated digits, so '7' appears in 77 \u2192 two 7s. For 3-digit multiples of 11: aba form where 2a \u2261 b mod 11... hmm, 3-digit multiples of 11: digits d1 d2 d3 with d1 - d2 + d3 \u2261 0 mod 11. \n\nThis is getting complicated. I'll trust the canonical HumanEval values. They are:\n- fizz_buzz(50) == 0 \u2713 (verified by hand)\n- fizz_buzz(78) == 2 \u2713 (verified by hand)\n- fizz_buzz(79) == 3 \u2713 (verified by hand)\n- fizz_buzz(100) == 3 \u2713 (verified by hand)\n- fizz_buzz(200) == 6 \u2713 (verified by hand)\n- fizz_buzz(4000) == 192 (canonical)\n- fizz_buzz(10000) == 639 (canonical)\n- fizz_buzz(99999) == 8026 (canonical)\n- fizz_buzz(0) == 0 \u2713\n\nI'll include 4000 and 10000 as they're canonical. Actually, to be extra safe, maybe I should limit to values I've verified by hand plus the canonical ones. The canonical ones are from the official HumanEval test suite, which is the source of this problem (f036 = HumanEval/36). So they're definitely correct for this exact implementation.\n\nHmm wait, actually let me double-check that this f036 is HumanEval/36. HumanEval problem 36:\n\n```\ndef fizz_buzz(n: int):\n    \"\"\"Return the number of times the digit 7 appears in integers less than n which are divisible by 11 or 13.\n    >>> fizz_buzz(50)\n    0\n    >>> fizz_buzz(78)\n    2\n    >>> fizz_buzz(79)\n    3\n    >>> fizz_buzz(100)\n    3\n    >>> fizz_buzz(200)\n    6\n    >>> fizz_buzz(4000)\n    192\n    >>> fizz_buzz(10000)\n    639\n    >>> fizz_buzz(99999)\n    8026\n    >>> fizz_buzz(0)\n    0\n    \"\"\"\n```\n\nYes, and the canonical solution is exactly the code shown. So the docstring examples are correct.\n\nLet me verify 4000 == 192 with more care, since I want the tests to be correct. Actually, let me try to compute it systematically.\n\nI need to count occurrences of digit '7' in the concatenation of all multiples of 11 or 13 in [0, 4000).\n\nAlternative approach: count '7' digits in each number that is a multiple of 11 or 13.\n\nLet me organize by ranges.\n\nNumbers 0-99 that are multiples of 11 or 13:\nMultiples of 11: 0, 11, 22, 33, 44, 55, 66, 77, 88, 99\nMultiples of 13: 0, 13, 26, 39, 52, 65, 78, 91\nUnion: 0, 11, 13, 22, 26, 33, 39, 44, 52, 55, 65, 66, 77, 78, 88, 91 (16 numbers)\n'7' count: 77 \u2192 2, 78 \u2192 1. Total: 3.\n\nNumbers 100-199, multiples of 11: 110, 121, 132, 143, 154, 165, 176, 187, 198 (9 numbers)\nMultiples of 13: 104, 117, 130, 143, 156, 169, 182, 195 (8 numbers; 143 shared)\nUnion: 104, 110, 117, 121, 130, 132, 143, 154, 156, 165, 169, 176, 182, 187, 195, 198 (16 numbers)\n'7' count: 117 \u2192 1, 176 \u2192 1, 187 \u2192 1. Total: 3.\n\nNumbers 200-299, multiples of 11: 209, 220, 231, 242, 253, 264, 275, 286, 297 (9)\nMultiples of 13: 208, 221, 234, 247, 260, 273, 286, 299 (8; 286 shared)\nUnion: 208, 209, 220, 221, 231, 234, 242, 247, 253, 260, 264, 273, 275, 286, 297, 299 (16)\n'7' count: 247 \u2192 1, 273 \u2192 1, 275 \u2192 1, 297 \u2192 1. Total: 4.\n\nNumbers 300-399, multiples of 11: 308, 319, 330, 341, 352, 363, 374, 385, 396 (9)\nMultiples of 13: 312, 325, 338, 351, 364, 377, 390 (7; check: 13\u00d724=312, 13\u00d725=325, 13\u00d726=338, 13\u00d727=351, 13\u00d728=364, 13\u00d729=377, 13\u00d730=390) (7)\nUnion: 308, 312, 319, 325, 330, 338, 341, 351, 352, 363, 364, 374, 377, 385, 390, 396 (16)\n'7' count: 374 \u2192 1, 377 \u2192 2. Total: 3.\n\nNumbers 400-499, multiples of 11: 407, 418, 429, 440, 451, 462, 473, 484, 495 (9)\nMultiples of 13: 403, 416, 429, 442, 455, 468, 481, 494 (8; 429 shared)\nUnion: 403, 407, 416, 418, 429, 440, 442, 451, 455, 462, 468, 473, 481, 484, 494, 495 (16)\n'7' count: 407 \u2192 1, 473 \u2192 1. Total: 2.\n\nNumbers 500-599, multiples of 11: 506, 517, 528, 539, 550, 561, 572, 583, 594 (9)\nMultiples of 13: 507, 520, 533, 546, 559, 572, 585, 598 (8; 572 shared)\nUnion: 506, 507, 517, 520, 528, 533, 539, 546, 550, 559, 561, 572, 583, 585, 594, 598 (16)\n'7' count: 507 \u2192 1, 517 \u2192 1, 572 \u2192 1. Total: 3.\n\nNumbers 600-699, multiples of 11: 605, 616, 627, 638, 649, 660, 671, 682, 693 (9)\nMultiples of 13: 611, 624, 637, 650, 663, 676, 689 (7; 13\u00d747=611, 13\u00d748=624, 13\u00d749=637, 13\u00d750=650, 13\u00d751=663, 13\u00d752=676, 13\u00d753=689)\nUnion: 605, 611, 616, 624, 627, 637, 638, 649, 650, 660, 663, 671, 676, 682, 689, 693 (16)\n'7' count: 627 \u2192 1, 637 \u2192 1, 671 \u2192 1, 676 \u2192 1. Total: 4.\n\nNumbers 700-799, multiples of 11: 704, 715, 726, 737, 748, 759, 770, 781, 792 (9)\nMultiples of 13: 702, 715, 728, 741, 754, 767, 780, 793 (8; 715 shared)\nUnion: 702, 704, 715, 726, 728, 737, 741, 748, 754, 759, 767, 770, 780, 781, 792, 793 (16)\n'7' count: every number starts with 7 \u2192 16, plus 737 \u2192 +1, 767 \u2192 +1, 770 \u2192 +2, 77? Let me check each: 702 (1), 704 (1), 715 (1), 726 (1), 728 (1), 737 (2), 741 (1), 748 (1), 754 (1), 759 (1), 767 (2), 770 (2), 780 (1), 781 (1), 792 (1), 793 (1). Sum: 1+1+1+1+1+2+1+1+1+1+2+2+1+1+1+1 = 18. Total: 18.\n\nNumbers 800-899, multiples of 11: 803, 814, 825, 836, 847, 858, 869, 880, 891 (9)\nMultiples of 13: 806, 819, 832, 845, 858, 871, 884, 897 (8; 858 shared)\nUnion: 803, 806, 814, 819, 825, 832, 836, 845, 847, 858, 869, 871, 880, 884, 891, 897 (16)\n'7' count: 847 \u2192 1, 871 \u2192 1, 897 \u2192 1. Total: 3.\n\nNumbers 900-999, multiples of 11: 902, 913, 924, 935, 946, 957, 968, 979, 990 (9)\nMultiples of 13: 910, 923, 936, 949, 962, 975, 988 (7)\nUnion: 902, 910, 913, 923, 924, 935, 936, 946, 949, 957, 962, 968, 975, 979, 988, 990 (16)\n'7' count: 957 \u2192 1, 975 \u2192 1, 979 \u2192 1. Total: 3.\n\nSo 0-999: 3 + 3 + 4 + 3 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 18 + 3 + 3 = 46.\n\nNumbers 1000-3999: each is a 4-digit number 1abc. Multiples of 11 or 13 in [1000, 4000).\n\nMultiples of 11 in [1000, 4000): 1001 (11\u00d791) to 3993 (11\u00d7363). Count: 363 - 91 + 1 = 273.\nMultiples of 13 in [1000, 4000): 1001 (13\u00d777) to 3991 (13\u00d7307). Count: 307 - 77 + 1 = 231.\nMultiples of 143 in [1000, 4000): 1001 (143\u00d77) to 3861 (143\u00d727). Count: 27 - 7 + 1 = 21.\nUnion: 273 + 231 - 21 = 483 numbers.\n\nNow count '7' digits among these 483 four-digit numbers. Hmm, this is the hard part. Let me think of a smarter way.\n\nTotal '7' count = sum over multiples m in [1000, 4000) of (number of '7' digits in m).\n\nLet me count by position: thousands digit, hundreds digit, tens digit, units digit.\n\nThousands digit: all numbers are 1xxx, 2xxx, 3xxx \u2014 never 7. So 0 from thousands.\n\nHundreds digit: count multiples of 11 or 13 in [1000,4000) with hundreds digit 7, i.e., in ranges [1700,1800), [2700,2800), [3700,3800).\n\nMultiples of 11 in [1700, 1800): 11\u00d7155 = 1705, ..., 11\u00d7163 = 1793. Count: 163 - 155 + 1 = 9.\nMultiples of 13 in [1700, 1800): 13\u00d7131 = 1703, ..., 13\u00d7138 = 1794. Count: 138 - 131 + 1 = 8.\nMultiples of 143 in [1700, 1800): 143\u00d712 = 1716. Count: 1.\nUnion: 9 + 8 - 1 = 16.\n\nMultiples of 11 in [2700, 2800): 11\u00d7246 = 2706, ..., 11\u00d7254 = 2794. Count: 9.\nMultiples of 13 in [2700, 2800): 13\u00d7208 = 2704, ..., 13\u00d7215 = 2795. Count: 8.\nMultiples of 143 in [2700, 2800): 143\u00d719 = 2717. Count: 1.\nUnion: 9 + 8 - 1 = 16.\n\nMultiples of 11 in [3700, 3800): 11\u00d7337 = 3707, ..., 11\u00d7345 = 3795. Count: 9.\nMultiples of 13 in [3700, 3800): 13\u00d7285 = 3705, ..., 13\u00d7292 = 3796. Count: 8.\nMultiples of 143 in [3700, 3800): 143\u00d726 = 3718. Count: 1.\nUnion: 16.\n\nHundreds digit '7' count: 16 \u00d7 3 = 48.\n\nTens digit: count multiples with tens digit 7, i.e., numbers of form x y 7 z where the number \u2261 0 mod 11 or 13. For each hundreds-block [ab00, ab00+100), count multiples of 11 or 13 in [ab70, ab80).\n\nFor multiples of 11: in any 100 consecutive integers, there are either 9 or 10 multiples of 11 (since 100/11 \u2248 9.09). In a specific 10-wide window [ab70, ab80), the count of multiples of 11 is either 0 or 1 (10 < 11). Similarly for 13: 0 or 1.\n\nSo for each of the 30 hundreds-blocks (hundreds digit 0-2 for thousands 1-3... wait, thousands 1, 2, 3 and hundreds 0-9 \u2192 30 blocks), count multiples of 11 in [ab70, ab80) plus multiples of 13 in [ab70, ab80) minus multiples of 143 in [ab70, ab80).\n\nMultiples of 11 in [ab70, ab80): the number 11k \u2261 ab70 mod 100... Let me think: multiples of 11 mod 100 cycle with period 11: 0, 11, 22, ..., 99, 0, ... Actually multiples of 11 mod 100: 11k mod 100 for k = 0..: 0, 11, 22, 33, 44, 55, 66, 77, 88, 99, 10, 21, 32, ... The last two digits of multiples of 11 take values \u2261 0 mod 11 (mod 100)? No wait: 11k mod 100. Since gcd(11, 100) = 1, 11k mod 100 cycles through all residues as k ranges over 0..99. So in any window of 10 consecutive integers [ab70, ab80), exactly... hmm, the multiples of 11 in a window of 10 consecutive integers: since 11 > 10, there's at most 1, and there's 1 iff the window contains a multiple of 11. The window [ab70, ab80) contains a multiple of 11 iff there exists k with ab70 \u2264 11k < ab80, i.e., 11k mod 100 \u2208 [70, 80). Since 11k mod 100 hits every residue class... over k = 0..99, 11k mod 100 takes each value exactly once. So for the tens digit to be 7: 11k mod 100 \u2208 {70, 71, ..., 79}. That's 10 values out of 100, so 10% of multiples of 11 have tens digit 7? Wait, but that's not right either \u2014 11k mod 100 for k = 0..99 gives all 100 residues, so exactly 10 values of k (out of every 100 consecutive k) give tens digit 7. Hmm, but that means 10% of multiples of 11 have tens digit 7? Let me sanity check with the 3-digit case: multiples of 11 in [100, 1000): 110 to 990, that's 81 multiples. Tens digit 7: numbers ending in 7x: 176, 187? No wait, tens digit 7 means the number is like x77? No \u2014 tens digit is the second-to-last digit. 176: digits 1,7,6 \u2014 tens digit is 7. Numbers with tens digit 7: 170-179, 270-279, ..., 970-979. Multiples of 11 in these: 176, 187? No, 187 has tens digit 8. Let me list: 176 (1,7,6 \u2014 tens 7 \u2713), 275, 374, 473, 572, 671, 770, 781? No, 781 tens digit is 8. Hmm wait: 770 \u2192 digits 7,7,0, tens digit 7 \u2713. 781 \u2192 7,8,1, tens 8 \u2717. Let me list multiples of 11 with tens digit 7 in [100,1000): 176, 275, 374, 473, 572, 671, 770, 869, 968. That's 9. And 81 multiples total \u2192 9/81 = 1/9 \u2248 11.1%. Hmm, not exactly 10%.\n\nOK here's the thing: 11k mod 100 cycles through all residues with period 100 in k. Multiples of 11 in [100, 1000) correspond to k in [10, 91) (since 11\u00d710 = 110, 11\u00d790 = 990, so k = 10..90, 81 values). For tens digit 7: 11k mod 100 \u2208 [70, 80). 11k mod 100 = (11k) mod 100. Let me solve: 11k \u2261 r mod 100 where r \u2208 [70, 80). k \u2261 11^{-1} r mod 100. 11 \u00d7 91 = 1001 \u2261 1 mod 100, so 11^{-1} = 91. k \u2261 91r mod 100. For r = 70: k \u2261 91\u00d770 = 6370 \u2261 70 mod 100. For r = 71: 91\u00d771 = 6461 \u2261 61. r=72: 91\u00d772 = 6552 \u2261 52. r=73: 6643 \u2261 43. r=74: 6734 \u2261 34. r=75: 6825 \u2261 25. r=76: 6916 \u2261 16. r=77: 7007 \u2261 7. r=78: 7098 \u2261 98. r=79: 7189 \u2261 89.\n\nSo k mod 100 \u2208 {70, 61, 52, 43, 34, 25, 16, 7, 98, 89} for tens digit 7. In k \u2208 [10, 91): values 16, 25, 34, 43, 52, 61, 70, 89 \u2192 8 values? Wait: 7 < 10, 98 \u2265 91, 89 \u2265 91? 89 < 91 \u2713. So k \u2208 {16, 25, 34, 43, 52, 61, 70, 89} \u2192 8 values. Hmm, but I listed 9 numbers above: 176 (k=16), 275 (k=25), 374 (k=34), 473 (k=43), 572 (k=52), 671 (k=61), 770 (k=70), 869 (k=79)? Wait, 869 = 11 \u00d7 79. 11 \u00d7 79 = 869. k=79. But 79 is not in my set {16, 25, 34, 43, 52, 61, 70, 89}. Let me recheck: 869 mod 100 = 69, tens digit 6, not 7! I made an error. 869: digits 8, 6, 9. Tens digit is 6. Oops. And 968 = 11 \u00d7 88, 968 mod 100 = 68, tens digit 6. I made errors in my listing.\n\nLet me redo: multiples of 11 with tens digit 7 in [100, 1000): k such that 11k \u2208 [100, 1000) and (11k // 10) % 10 == 7. From the k set {16, 25, 34, 43, 52, 61, 70, 89}: 11\u00d716 = 176 \u2713, 11\u00d725 = 275 \u2713, 11\u00d734 = 374 \u2713, 11\u00d743 = 473 \u2713, 11\u00d752 = 572 \u2713, 11\u00d761 = 671 \u2713, 11\u00d770 = 770 \u2713, 11\u00d789 = 979 \u2713. So 8 numbers: 176, 275, 374, 473, 572, 671, 770, 979.\n\nOK so this positional counting approach works but is getting complex. Let me just carefully count for [1000, 4000).\n\nActually, let me simplify: I'll count '7' digits by position for the 483 numbers in [1000, 4000) that are multiples of 11 or 13.\n\nThousands digit: never 7 (numbers are 1xxx, 2xxx, 3xxx). Count: 0.\n\nHundreds digit = 7: numbers in [1700, 1800) \u222a [2700, 2800) \u222a [3700, 3800) that are multiples of 11 or 13. Computed above: 16 + 16 + 16 = 48. Each contributes exactly one '7' (the hundreds digit). Count: 48.\n\nWait, I should double check those counts.\n\nMultiples of 11 in [1700, 1800): 1700/11 = 154.5..., so first multiple \u2265 1700 is 11 \u00d7 155 = 1705. Last < 1800: 11 \u00d7 163 = 1793 (11 \u00d7 164 = 1804). So k = 155..163 \u2192 9 multiples. \u2713\n\nMultiples of 13 in [1700, 1800): 1700/13 = 130.77, first is 13 \u00d7 131 = 1703. Last < 1800: 13 \u00d7 138 = 1794 (13 \u00d7 139 = 1807). k = 131..138 \u2192 8. \u2713\n\nMultiples of 143 in [1700, 1800): 143 \u00d7 11 = 1573, 143 \u00d7 12 = 1716, 143 \u00d7 13 = 1859. So just 1716 \u2192 1. \u2713\nUnion: 9 + 8 - 1 = 16. \u2713\n\n[2700, 2800): 2700/11 = 245.45, first 11 \u00d7 246 = 2706. Last: 11 \u00d7 254 = 2794 (11 \u00d7 255 = 2805). \u2192 9.\n2700/13 = 207.7, first 13 \u00d7 208 = 2704. Last: 13 \u00d7 215 = 2795 (13 \u00d7 216 = 2808). \u2192 8.\n143 \u00d7 18 = 2574, 143 \u00d7 19 = 2717, 143 \u00d7 20 = 2860. \u2192 1 (2717).\nUnion: 16. \u2713\n\n[3700, 3800): 3700/11 = 336.36, first 11 \u00d7 337 = 3707. Last: 11 \u00d7 345 = 3795 (11 \u00d7 346 = 3806). \u2192 9.\n3700/13 = 284.6, first 13 \u00d7 285 = 3705. Last: 13 \u00d7 292 = 3796 (13 \u00d7 293 = 3809). \u2192 8.\n143 \u00d7 25 = 3575, 143 \u00d7 26 = 3718, 143 \u00d7 27 = 3861. \u2192 1 (3718).\nUnion: 16. \u2713\n\nHundreds digit total: 48.\n\nTens digit = 7: numbers in [ab70, ab80) for ab \u2208 {10, 11, ..., 39} (i.e., 1000-3999), that are multiples of 11 or 13.\n\nFor each block [ab00, ab00+100), the window [ab70, ab80) contains at most one multiple of 11 and at most one multiple of 13.\n\nMultiples of 11 in [ab70, ab80): 11k mod 100 \u2208 [70, 80) \u2192 k mod 100 \u2208 {70, 61, 52, 43, 34, 25, 16, 7, 98, 89} (computed above). For ab from 10 to 39: 11k \u2208 [ab70, ab80) means k \u2208 [ab70/11, ab80/11). Alternatively: for each ab, check if any k \u2261 r (mod 100) with r in the set gives 11k \u2208 [ab70, ab80).\n\nSimpler: for each ab in 10..39, the window is [100\u00d7ab + 70, 100\u00d7ab + 80). A multiple of 11 is in this window iff 11k mod 100 \u2208 [70, 80) and floor(11k/100) = ab. Since 11k mod 100 determines k mod 100, and each ab corresponds to k in a range of ~9 values... Let me just directly compute for each ab whether there's a multiple of 11 in [ab70, ab80).\n\nThe multiples of 11 near ab70: 11k where k = round(ab70/11)... Let me compute 11k mod 1000 pattern? Hmm, alternative: multiples of 11 in [ab70, ab80): the number ab70 + j for j = 0..9. ab70 + j \u2261 0 mod 11. ab70 mod 11: ab70 = 100\u00d7ab + 70. 100 \u2261 1 mod 11, so ab70 \u2261 ab + 70 \u2261 ab + 4 mod 11 (since 70 = 66 + 4). So ab70 + j \u2261 ab + 4 + j mod 11. We need ab + 4 + j \u2261 0 mod 11 for some j \u2208 [0, 10). j = (-ab - 4) mod 11. This j must be \u2264 9. So there's a multiple of 11 in the window iff (-ab - 4) mod 11 \u2264 9, i.e., (-ab - 4) mod 11 \u2260 10.\n\n(-ab - 4) mod 11 = 10 iff ab + 4 \u2261 1 mod 11 iff ab \u2261 8 mod 11 (since -ab - 4 \u2261 10 \u2192 ab + 4 \u2261 -10 \u2261 1 mod 11 \u2192 ab \u2261 -3 \u2261 8 mod 11).\n\nSo for ab \u2262 8 (mod 11), there's exactly one multiple of 11 in [ab70, ab80); for ab \u2261 8 (mod 11), none.\n\nab ranges over 10..39: ab \u2261 8 mod 11 \u2192 ab \u2208 {19, 30} (8, 19, 30, 41 \u2014 within 10..39: 19, 30). So 28 blocks have a multiple of 11 with tens digit 7, 2 blocks don't. Count of multiples of 11 with tens digit 7 in [1000, 4000): 28.\n\nWait, but I need to double check: j = (-ab - 4) mod 11 must be in [0, 10), i.e., j \u2264 9. j = 10 means no multiple. j = (-ab-4) mod 11 = 10 iff ab \u2261 8 mod 11. Let me verify with an example: ab = 19: window [1970, 1980). Multiples of 11: 11 \u00d7 179 = 1969, 11 \u00d7 180 = 1980. 1980 is not < 1980, so none in [1970, 1980). \u2713 (1969 < 1970.) \n\nab = 30: window [3070, 3080). 11 \u00d7 279 = 3069, 11 \u00d7 280 = 3080. None in [3070, 3080). \u2713\n\nGreat, so 28 multiples of 11 with tens digit 7.\n\nMultiples of 13 in [ab70, ab80): ab70 + j \u2261 0 mod 13. ab70 = 100ab + 70. 100 mod 13 = 9 (100 = 96 + 4? no: 13 \u00d7 7 = 91, 100 - 91 = 9). So 100ab + 70 \u2261 9ab + 70 mod 13. 70 mod 13 = 70 - 65 = 5. So ab70 \u2261 9ab + 5 mod 13. Need 9ab + 5 + j \u2261 0 mod 13, j \u2208 [0, 10). j = (-9ab - 5) mod 13. Need j \u2264 9, i.e., j \u2260 10, 11, 12.\n\nj = (-9ab - 5) mod 13 \u2208 {10, 11, 12} iff 9ab + 5 \u2261 3, 2, 1 mod 13 (since -x mod 13 = 10 \u2192 x \u2261 3; = 11 \u2192 x \u2261 2; = 12 \u2192 x \u2261 1). So no multiple of 13 iff 9ab + 5 \u2261 1, 2, or 3 mod 13, i.e., 9ab \u2261 -4, -3, -2 \u2261 9, 10, 11 mod 13.\n\n9ab mod 13: 9 \u00d7 ab mod 13. 9^{-1} mod 13: 9 \u00d7 3 = 27 \u2261 1 mod 13, so 9^{-1} = 3. ab \u2261 3 \u00d7 {9, 10, 11} = {27, 30, 33} \u2261 {1, 4, 7} mod 13.\n\nSo no multiple of 13 in the window iff ab \u2261 1, 4, or 7 mod 13.\n\nab \u2208 10..39: ab \u2261 1 mod 13: 14, 27, 40\u2192 no, so {14, 27}. ab \u2261 4 mod 13: 17, 30. ab \u2261 7 mod 13: 20, 33. So blocks without a multiple of 13: {14, 27, 17, 30, 20, 33} \u2192 6 blocks. 30 - 6 = 24 blocks have a multiple of 13 with tens digit 7.\n\nMultiples of 143 in [ab70, ab80): 143k. 143 mod 100 = 43. Window width 10 < 143, so at most one. 143k \u2208 [ab70, ab80): 143k mod 100 \u2208 [70, 80) and floor(143k / 100) = ab. 143k mod 100 = 43k mod 100. 43k mod 100 \u2208 [70, 80): 43k \u2261 r, r \u2208 [70, 80). 43^{-1} mod 100: 43 \u00d7 7 = 301 \u2261 1 mod 100. So 43^{-1} = 7. k \u2261 7r mod 100. r = 70: 490 \u2261 90. r = 71: 497 \u2261 97. r = 72: 504 \u2261 4. r = 73: 511 \u2261 11. r = 74: 518 \u2261 18. r = 75: 525 \u2261 25. r = 76: 532 \u2261 32. r = 77: 539 \u2261 39. r = 78: 546 \u2261 46. r = 79: 553 \u2261 53.\n\nSo k mod 100 \u2208 {90, 97, 4, 11, 18, 25, 32, 39, 46, 53}. 143k with k mod 100 = 90: 143 \u00d7 90 = 12870. Hmm, this is for k in range... 143k \u2208 [1000, 4000) \u2192 k \u2208 [7, 28). k values: 7..27. k mod 100 \u2208 {90, 97, 4, 11, 18, 25, 32, 39, 46, 53} \u2192 within 7..27: {11, 18, 25}. So k = 11, 18, 25 \u2192 143 \u00d7 11 = 1573, 143 \u00d7 18 = 2574, 143 \u00d7 25 = 3575. Tens digits: 1573 \u2192 7 \u2713, 2574 \u2192 7 \u2713, 3575 \u2192 7 \u2713. So 3 multiples of 143 with tens digit 7 in [1000, 4000).\n\nBut wait \u2014 these are also counted in the multiples of 11 and 13 counts. 1573 = 11 \u00d7 143 = 13 \u00d7 121. Yes, 1573 is a multiple of both 11 and 13. So in my counts of 28 (for 11) and 24 (for 13), 1573 is counted in both. Similarly 2574 = 11 \u00d7 234? 11 \u00d7 234 = 2574 \u2713, and 13 \u00d7 198 = 2574 \u2713. And 3575 = 11 \u00d7 325 \u2713, 13 \u00d7 275 = 3575 \u2713. So all 3 are multiples of both.\n\nBy inclusion-exclusion, count of numbers with tens digit 7 that are multiples of 11 or 13 = 28 + 24 - 3 = 49.\n\nHmm wait, but I need to be careful: the multiples of 11 with tens digit 7 \u2014 are any of them also multiples of 13 but not counted in my 24? The 24 count includes all multiples of 13 with tens digit 7, including those that are also multiples of 11. And the 28 includes all multiples of 11 with tens digit 7. The intersection is multiples of 143 with tens digit 7 = 3. So union = 28 + 24 - 3 = 49. \u2713\n\nTens digit total: 49.\n\nUnits digit = 7: numbers \u2261 7 mod 10 that are multiples of 11 or 13.\n\nMultiples of 11 ending in 7: 11k \u2261 7 mod 10 \u2192 k \u2261 7 mod 10 (since 11 \u2261 1 mod 10). So k \u2208 {7, 17, 27, ...}. 11k \u2208 [1000, 4000) \u2192 k \u2208 [91, 364). k \u2261 7 mod 10: 97, 107, ..., 357. Count: (357 - 97)/10 + 1 = 27. So 27 multiples of 11 ending in 7.\n\nMultiples of 13 ending in 7: 13k \u2261 7 mod 10 \u2192 3k \u2261 7 